## Why This Is Asked

Tests multi-state DP design to enforce a direction-run constraint with obstacles, requiring careful state construction and both memoized and tabular implementations.

## Key Concepts

- DP with extra dimensions for last direction and run-length
- Top-down memoization and bottom-up tabulation
- Modulo arithmetic and handling blocked cells; boundary cases when L is large or grid small

## Code Example

```javascript
function countPaths(grid, L){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length, MOD = 1000000007;
  if(grid[0][0] || grid[n-1][m-1]) return 0;
  const memo = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array.from({length:m},()=>Array.from({length:2},()=>Array(L+1).fill(-1))));
  const endI=n-1, endJ=m-1;
  function dfs(i,j,dir,run){
    if(i===endI && j===endJ) return 1;
    const val = memo[i][j][dir][run];
    if(val!==-1) return val;
    let res = 0;
    // right
    if(j+1<m){
      if((dir===0 && run<L) || dir===1){
        res = (res + dfs(i, j+1, 0, dir===0 ? run+1 : 1)) % MOD;
      }
    }
    // down
    if(i+1<n){
      if((dir===1 && run<L) || dir===0){
        res = (res + dfs(i+1, j, 1, dir===1 ? run+1 : 1)) % MOD;
      }
    }
    memo[i][j][dir][run] = res;
    return res;
  }
  let ans = (dfs(0,0,0,1) + dfs(0,0,1,1)) % MOD;
  return ans;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reduce memory to O(n m)?
- What changes if you allow a diagonal move on occasion?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical Trie usage for autocomplete, encouraging explicit design choices and complexity reasoning.

## Key Concepts

- Trie node with 26-child array and isWord flag
- DFS enumeration in lexicographic order
- Edge cases: empty string, duplicates, limit handling, memory usage

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() { this.next = new Array(26).fill(null); this.isWord = false; }
}
class Trie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insert(w) { let p = this.root; for (const ch of w) { const i = ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if (!p.next[i]) p.next[i] = new TrieNode(); p = p.next[i]; } p.isWord = true; }
  search(w) { let p = this.root; for (const ch of w) { const i = ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if (!p.next[i]) return false; p = p.next[i]; } return p.isWord; }
  startsWith(pfx) { let p = this.root; for (const ch of pfx) { const i = ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if (!p.next[i]) return false; p = p.next[i]; } return true; }
  listWithPrefix(pfx, limit) { const res = []; let p = this.root; for (const ch of pfx) { const i = ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if (!p.next[i]) return res; p = p.next[i]; } const dfs = (node, prefix) => { if (res.length >= limit) return; if (node.isWord) res.push(prefix); for (let c = 0; c < 26; c++) { const child = node.next[c]; if (child) dfs(child, prefix + String.fromCharCode(97+c)); } }; dfs(p, pfx); return res; }
}
```

